  "stopped_because": "concluded",
  "narrative": "Higher late\u2011night phone use is linked to larger gambling stakes and net losses. Across four night\u2011use bands, mean monthly stake rises from\u202f\u00a39.43 (rare) to\u202f\u00a312.91 (occasional),\u202f\u00a317.56 (regular) and\u202f\u00a348.66 (heavy) (step\u202f1). Mean monthly net loss shows a similar pattern:\u202f\u00a30.80 (rare),\u202f\u00a31.69 (occasional),\u202f\u00a32.07 (regular) and\u202f\u00a38.03 (heavy) (step\u202f6). The correlation between the number of late\u2011night phone\u2011use sessions and stake is modest but positive (r\u202f=\u202f0.082, p\u202f\u2248\u202f0.0, n\u202f=\u202f72\u202f000) (step\u202f2). A Gaussian GLM adjusting for income band estimates that, relative to the reference band, occasional use adds\u202f\u00a33.52, regular use adds\u202f\u00a38.14 and heavy use adds\u202f\u00a339.36 to expected stake (step\u202f4). Cell\u2011wise means from the same model show heavy users in the \u00a320\u201135\u202fk band stake about\u202f\u00a366.57 on average, while heavy users in the >\u00a380\u202fk band stake about\u202f\u00a349.47 (step\u202f4 companions). Requests for models additionally adjusted for employment, and for income\u202f+\u202femployment, were refused by the safe\u2011outputs gateway (steps\u202f3 and\u202f5). Overall, the evidence supports a positive association between late\u2011night phone use and gambling expenditure.",
  "unsupported_figures": [
    "35"
  ]
